WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:05.050
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello,
I'm Dustin Kirkland,

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.133
Product Manager at Google.

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:09.370
And I'm joined here by
my colleague Sebastien

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.425
Goasguen at Bitnami,
where he leads Kubernetes.

00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:14.050
Sebastien, can you
tell me a little bit

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.630
about what you're hearing
from users and customers

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.999
about Kubernetes in
Bitnami's sphere?

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:21.040
SEBASTIEN GOASGUEN: Sure.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.370
At Bitnami, as you said, I lead
all the community's efforts

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:25.600
open source.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.060
So we work on
application packaging

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.850
specifically for Kubernetes,
so of course, containers.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.970
But then we package, and
we deliver on Kubernetes.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:37.850
GKE, of course, is one
of the target platforms.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.330
And it's still a little
bit early, you know.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.040
I mean, the conference
is booming--

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.090
4,000 people.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.430
But it's still
early in the space,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.060
where users are migrating
to Microservices

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.930
and Cloud-native applications.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.810
So they need a lot
of help in first,

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.330
understanding the
entire space, and then

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.980
understanding how
their application is

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.380
going to look like in
Kubernetes on that platform.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.810
So we help them
do that, and then

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.460
we help them package those apps,
make the good choices in terms

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.330
of containerizing them, using
things like Helm charts,

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.004
or maybe GitHub's
pipeline to deploy.

00:01:14.004 --> 00:01:14.920
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.000
So you've really seen this shift
in development methodologies

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.779
from the developer's
perspective.

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:22.570
SEBASTIEN GOASGUEN:
Yeah, and personally, I

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.640
like to think of
it also as-- it's

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.070
more of a general
shift with DevOps

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.400
and being able to be
extremely quick to market,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.490
to react extremely
quickly, compared

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.000
to a more traditional type
of application development,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.010
where that's slower and
people need months or years

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.090
to deploy apps.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.070
Here, we see people
realizing that they

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.410
need to go extremely quickly.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.800
So that's where Microservices,
containers, and then a platform

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.234
like Kube makes it extremely
easy, with rolling updates.

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:56.650
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
CI/CD, of course.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.470
SEBASTIEN GOASGUEN: CI/CD, of
course, which is integrated.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.570
But interestingly, we
have to be realistic.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.610
A lot of people are
still not there.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.210
So yes, CI/CD.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.770
But we need to help those people
who are maybe not quite there

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.650
yet with their pipeline, with
their software development

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.572
pipeline, get them on board with
this new wave of doing apps.

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:17.530
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.613
And where do you see the
challenges are right now?

00:02:19.613 --> 00:02:21.310
Where are people
stubbing their toes?

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.990
SEBASTIEN GOASGUEN:
Well, so you need

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:26.140
to help them understand the
impact of those technologies.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.230
I always say, adopting a
tech just because that's

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.160
the new thing, going Kubernetes
just because Kubernetes

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.520
is really hot right now--

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.180
that's not a good reason.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.090
So they really need to
understand that they

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.890
need a software factory.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.250
They need developers on
board with using a version

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.770
control, of course, but
then automatically building

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.190
containers, understanding
the manifests for Kubernetes.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.400
So it's an entire
lifecycle of software,

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.490
lifecycle of the application,
that they need to embrace.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.190
And we need to start
at the beginning, which

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.030
is the dev, of course.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.720
And then we need to
approach the operators,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.690
because it's a big
change for the operators

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.340
of the infrastructure.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.430
They need to be on board with
adopting the Kubernetes API

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.310
to manage this infrastructure.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.670
So it's an entire effort
there of transformation

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:16.960
for [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:17.980
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.140
So you're quite involved
in the community.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.819
And you've certainly
seen and met

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:23.860
a lot of people around
Kubernetes over the years.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.710
Who are some of the people
that inspire you, maybe you

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.330
want to give a
shout out to or give

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.660
a thanks to on camera here?

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.810
SEBASTIEN GOASGUEN: I
started in Kubernetes 2014,

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.520
when I wrote the
"Docker Cookbook."

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.470
So I met Joe Bida
remotely, and he

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.790
helped me write some of
the Kubernetes content

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.300
before he went GA.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.170
So I can't say that
I was there early.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.640
But I don't work on core.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.630
I really work on the edge,
because I created a startup

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.310
which was acquired by Bitnami.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.190
So I'm really on the edge, not
really contributing to core

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.410
per se.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.780
But the people I
really admire, Joe Bida

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.560
is one of them--
extremely smart,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.376
of course-- which was at Google
before and worked on GCE.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.530
And then I have to say
that Brian Grant is

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.520
quite impressive to
me as a smooth guy,

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.410
quiet but extremely sharp.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.110
And when you point out
a technical problem,

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:18.670
he brings up a GitHub issue
or a PR with its number,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:19.867
so it's quite impressive.

00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:21.700
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Well,
thank you, Sebastien.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.574
Appreciate your time.

00:04:22.574 --> 00:04:24.430
Yeah, cheers.

